1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:06,625
The brush clears a bronze helmet, crested and curved. It is Greek in style.

2
00:00:06,625 --> 00:00:09,986
Yet it is a find from the north-west of the subcontinent.

3
00:00:09,986 --> 00:00:18,459
Two great empires, Persian and Greek, reached this land before the Mauryas. What was a Greek helmet doing in India?

4
00:00:33,950 --> 00:00:40,699
How do we know? Persian inscriptions at Behistun and Persepolis name a land called Hindush.

5
00:00:40,699 --> 00:00:45,554
Greek writers, such as Arrian, wrote about Alexander and what he saw.

6
00:00:45,554 --> 00:00:49,654
They were written long after, so they have to be read with care.

7
00:01:03,650 --> 00:01:05,663
First came the Persians.

8
00:01:05,663 --> 00:01:15,399
About 518 BCE, Darius the First, of the Achaemenid empire, took parts of the north-west, which his inscriptions call Hindush.

9
00:01:15,399 --> 00:01:22,100
It became a Persian province, a satrapy, and paid tribute in gold. One result was a new script.

10
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:31,042
Kharosthi, written right to left, grew out of the Aramaic script used by Persian clerks. Then came Alexander of Macedon.

11
00:01:31,042 --> 00:01:38,209
He crossed the Hindu Kush in 327 BCE. The king of Taxila, Ambhi, joined him.

12
00:01:38,209 --> 00:01:44,706
In 326 BCE he fought King Porus on the river Jhelum, which the Greeks called the Hydaspes.

13
00:01:44,706 --> 00:01:49,824
The battle was hard, and Alexander honoured Porus and left him as his ally.

14
00:01:49,824 --> 00:01:59,642
At the river Beas, his tired soldiers refused to march further. Alexander turned back, and died in Babylon in 323 BCE.

15
00:01:59,642 --> 00:02:07,465
His conquest left no lasting empire. But it opened new routes, and Greek and Indian ideas began to meet.

16
00:02:21,450 --> 00:02:27,046
Old name, new name. The Persians called the land by the river, Hindush.

17
00:02:27,046 --> 00:02:34,964
The Greeks dropped the first sound and said Indos, and later India. All three names come from the great river Sindhu.

18
00:02:48,950 --> 00:02:58,006
Carve this tip into memory. Darius took Hindush. Alexander stopped at the Beas. Persians first, Greeks second.

19
00:02:58,006 --> 00:03:01,902
Kharosthi from Persia, and a helmet from Greece.

20
00:03:15,883 --> 00:03:32,939
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Which Persian king added Hindush to his empire? The answer: Darius the First. Number two.

21
00:03:32,939 --> 00:03:44,835
On which river did Alexander fight Porus? The answer: the Jhelum, or Hydaspes. Number three.

22
00:03:44,835 --> 00:03:57,326
Which script grew from the Aramaic of Persian clerks? The answer: Kharosthi. Now one line for Mains.

23
00:03:57,326 --> 00:04:02,565
Assess the impact of the Persian and Macedonian invasions on India.

24
00:04:20,550 --> 00:04:28,529
The Greek helmet goes into its case. It is a reminder of the first meeting of two worlds. Three facts to take away.

25
00:04:28,529 --> 00:04:40,307
Crested bronze, Greek in style. Alexander crossed the Hindu Kush, 327 BCE. He fought Porus on the Jhelum, 326 BCE.

26
00:04:40,307 --> 00:04:43,858
Next gallery: The First Empire.

